Your knitr opencpu app is a good start, but small suggestion for improvement: In the current version of your app, the client (javascript) is pushing R code. This could potentially lead to problems, for example when the text contains quotes. The cool thing about shipping a webapp inside an R package is that there is a more natural place to put your R code. It is better to put any R code where it belongs: in your R package. For example, create an R function in your package:

```
knit2html <- function(text, ...){
  # remove explicit package names if they are imported into your package.
  mdtext <- knitr::knit(text=text);
  htmldoc <- markdown::markdownToHTML(text=mdtext, ...);
  htmlbody <- sub(".*<body>(.*)</body>.*", "\\\\1", htmldoc);
  return(htmlbody)
}
```

And then have your client call this function (instead of identity), directly passing on input text rather than wrapping it into R using javascript.

