when fig.align='center' is used together with subfigures/subcaptions, the LaTeX output only centers the contents of the subfloats, not the content of the overall figure which includes the subfloats:

```
\begin{knitrout}
\definecolor{shadecolor}{rgb}{0.969, 0.969, 0.969}\color{fgcolor}
\begin{figure}[h!]
\subfloat[one plot\label{fig:unnamed-chunk-21}]{

{\centering \includegraphics[width=0.4\textwidth]{figure/unnamed-chunk-21} 

}

}
\subfloat[the other one\label{fig:unnamed-chunk-22}]{

{\centering \includegraphics[width=0.4\textwidth]{figure/unnamed-chunk-22} 

}

}\caption[Overall caption]{Overall caption\label{fig:unnamed-chunk-2}}
```

Since the floats are never going to be wider than the images this means that the images are actually never going to be center-aligned, but simply clustered together against the left margin. To achieve the wanted behaviour there should be an extra \centering right after \begin{figure} and before the first \subfloat, as can be seen in examples here:
http://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/LaTeX/Floats,_Figures_and_Captions#Subfloats
http://tex.stackexchange.com/questions/81953/obtaining-the-behavior-of-both-subfigure-and-subcaptionbox-in-the-subcaption-pac

The fix should probably go here:
https://github.com/yihui/knitr/blob/36d7f372bf17d7623ab03badb315083cf64e6fde/R/hooks-latex.R#L87
although I'm not sure how exactly it will look like - the condition should definitely not be limited to standalone-figures, but does that mean that the if-statement can be dropped altogether?

